[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This document states the software requirements of the CAN bus component, the lowest part of the
communication layer. It sends and receives CAN frames on a named interface: vcan0 against the
simulated units in development, and the car's OBD-II port on the target.

The component does not configure the interface (bitrate, up or down state); the operating system
does that at installation. Bus-off handling belongs to the hardware stage and is not covered here.

Each requirement links to the system requirements it serves with a Parent relation.
Requirements tagged "derived" have no system-level parent: they come from design decisions
rather than from a system requirement, and their rationale explains why they exist.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_001
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus
TITLE: Classic CAN frames
STATEMENT: The bus component shall send and receive classic CAN data frames consisting of an identifier, a data length code (DLC) from 0 to 8, and up to 8 data bytes.
RATIONALE: The units' status and the vehicle's OBD-II responses arrive as CAN frames. CAN FD is out of scope.
VERIFICATION: Test
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_MON_001
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_VEH_001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_002
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Standard identifiers only
STATEMENT: The bus component shall send and deliver only frames with 11-bit standard identifiers, in the range 0x000 to 0x7FF, and shall not deliver received frames with 29-bit extended identifiers.
RATIONALE: Derived. The simulated units use standard identifiers, and the target vehicle is assumed to answer OBD-II requests on standard identifiers. The assumption is confirmed on the first connection to the car.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_003
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus
TITLE: Open by interface name and mode
STATEMENT: The bus component shall open a connection to a CAN interface given by name, in either simulation mode or real-vehicle mode, and shall report failure if the interface cannot be opened. The mode shall stay fixed until the bus is closed. Opening a bus that is already open shall fail without changing its state. A closed bus may be opened again.
RATIONALE: The same code runs against vcan0 and the car. Fixing the mode while open, and refusing a second open, mean the transmit restriction of SW_CAN_009 cannot be switched off at run time.
VERIFICATION: Test
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_MON_001
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_VEH_001
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_SAFE_001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_004
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Operations on a bus that is not open
STATEMENT: When the bus component is not open, send and receive requests shall fail without side effects.
RATIONALE: Derived. Calls made before open or after close must not cause undefined behaviour.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_005
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Close
STATEMENT: The bus component shall release its connection on close. Closing a bus that is not open shall have no effect.
RATIONALE: Derived. Shutdown must be possible from any state.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_006
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus
TITLE: Send
STATEMENT: When open, the bus component shall report success when a frame that meets SW_CAN_007 and SW_CAN_009 has been accepted for transmission by the CAN interface, and report failure otherwise. Success does not mean that the frame was acknowledged on the bus.
RATIONALE: OBD-II requests to the vehicle are sent as frames, and the sender must know whether each one left the component. Confirmation of transmission on the bus is not needed.
VERIFICATION: Test
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_VEH_001
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_VEH_002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_007
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Reject invalid frames on send
STATEMENT: The bus component shall reject, without transmitting, a frame whose DLC exceeds 8 or whose identifier exceeds 0x7FF.
RATIONALE: Derived. Invalid frames must never reach the bus.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_008
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Receive with timeout
STATEMENT: The bus component shall return the next received frame, or report that no frame arrived, within a timeout given by the caller.
RATIONALE: Derived. The I/O thread must be able to check for a stop request regularly, without busy-waiting or blocking forever.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_009
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, safety
TITLE: Transmit restriction in real-vehicle mode
STATEMENT: In real-vehicle mode, the bus component shall transmit only frames with identifier 0x7DF or 0x7E0 to 0x7E7, and shall reject any other frame without transmitting it.
RATIONALE: These are the 11-bit OBD-II request identifiers of ISO 15765-4. Enforcing them in the lowest layer means no fault above it can put another frame on the car's bus. The OBD-II client remains responsible for request content and for the rate limit of SYS_SAFE_002.
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_SAFE_001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_010
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Receive order
STATEMENT: The bus component shall deliver received frames in the order in which they were received.
RATIONALE: Derived. The E2E alive counter check depends on frames arriving in order.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_011
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Filter non-data frames
STATEMENT: The bus component shall not deliver remote frames or error frames to the caller.
RATIONALE: Derived. Only data frames carry the signals defined in the ICD.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_012
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Receive timestamp
STATEMENT: The bus component shall attach to every delivered frame the time at which the component read it from the CAN interface, taken from the system's monotonic clock.
RATIONALE: Derived. Using the same clock as the scheduler lets receive times be compared with cycle times, for example to detect messages that stopped arriving, and lets tests use the fake clock.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_013
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Dropped frame count
STATEMENT: The bus component shall provide the number of frames lost because its receive buffer was full, counted since it was opened. Frames not delivered because of SW_CAN_002 or SW_CAN_011 shall not be counted.
RATIONALE: Derived. Lost frames must be visible, never silent. Frames filtered on purpose are not losses.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_014
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: No dynamic allocation
STATEMENT: The bus component shall not allocate memory dynamically after it has been opened.
RATIONALE: Derived from the project coding rule. Allocation at run time can take unbounded time or fail.
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_015
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Concurrent send and receive
STATEMENT: The bus component shall support one thread receiving and one other thread sending at the same time.
RATIONALE: Derived from the architecture: the I/O thread receives while the logic thread sends. Open and close are not called while another thread sends or receives; the I/O thread is stopped before close.
VERIFICATION: Test

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_016
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus
TITLE: Send does not block
STATEMENT: The bus component shall return from a send request without waiting. If the frame cannot be accepted for transmission at once, the send shall report failure.
RATIONALE: The logic thread sends inside the cycle. Waiting for the interface could make the cycle overrun.
VERIFICATION: Test
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_LIGHT_001
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS_STATE_003

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SW_CAN_017
STATUS: Draft
TAGS: can_bus, derived
TITLE: Interface lost while open
STATEMENT: If the CAN interface becomes unavailable while the bus is open, send and receive requests shall report failure without blocking beyond the receive timeout, and the bus component shall report that the interface is unavailable.
RATIONALE: Derived. A lost interface, such as a disconnected CAN adapter, must be visible so that it can be handled as a fault, and must not hang the I/O thread.
VERIFICATION: Test
